You are executing a batch workflow for: {{task_description}}

This is a 3-phase execution model designed for complex, multi-step tasks that benefit from parallel agent delegation. Follow each phase precisely.

## Phase 1: Research and Plan

### 1.1 Task Analysis
Analyze the overall goal and decompose it:
- Identify all distinct sub-tasks and their dependencies
- Categorize sub-tasks by: research, implementation, testing, documentation, integration
- Determine which sub-tasks can run in parallel and which have sequential dependencies
- Estimate complexity for each sub-task (simple, medium, complex)

### 1.2 Workplan Generation
Create a structured workplan using the TodoWrite tool:
- Create todo items for each major milestone
- Label dependencies clearly (Item X depends on Item Y)
- Assign priority levels: high, medium, low
- Ensure the plan covers the entire task end-to-end with no gaps

### 1.3 Validation
Before proceeding to Phase 2:
- Verify the plan covers all requirements
- Confirm no circular dependencies exist
- Ensure the total number of sub-tasks is appropriate (5-30 for most tasks)
- Present the plan to the user for confirmation if the scope is large or ambiguous

## Phase 2: Parallel Agent Execution

### 2.1 Agent Spawning
For each independent sub-task identified in Phase 1:
- Use the Agent tool (task_create) to spawn a dedicated agent
- Provide each agent with a clear, self-contained prompt describing exactly its scope
- Include specific file paths, function names, and expected output format in each agent's prompt
- Set appropriate model/tool configurations per agent based on task type

### 2.2 Concurrency Management
- Spawn all independent agents simultaneously for maximum parallelism
- For dependent tasks, wait for prerequisite agents to complete before spawning downstream agents
- Monitor agent progress and resource utilization
- If an agent fails, assess whether to retry with adjusted parameters or handle the failure inline

### 2.3 Agent Communication
- Each agent works in isolation on its assigned scope
- Agents should NOT modify files that other agents are actively working on
- Use the file system as the communication channel (agents write results, coordinator reads them)

## Phase 3: Progress Tracking and Synthesis

### 3.1 Progress Monitoring
Maintain a live progress table tracking all agents:

| Agent ID | Task | Status | Files Changed | Result |
|----------|------|--------|---------------|--------|
| task_1   | ...  | running| -             | -      |
| task_2   | ...  | done   | 3 files       | OK     |

Update this table with each agent completion. Mark the corresponding todo items in Phase 1.

### 3.2 Result Aggregation
After all agents complete:
- Collect and review all agent outputs
- Verify consistency across agent results (no conflicting changes)
- Identify any gaps that were not covered
- Run integration validation: compilation check, test suite, linter

### 3.3 Final Synthesis
- Produce a unified summary of all changes made
- List all modified files with brief descriptions of changes
- Report any issues, warnings, or incomplete items
- If all tasks succeeded, declare the batch complete
- If some tasks failed, report which ones and suggest next steps

## Error Handling
- If an agent times out, retry with adjusted scope once
- If an agent produces invalid output, analyze and re-spawn with corrective instructions
- If dependencies cannot be resolved, surface the blocker clearly
- Never silently discard agent failures

## Output Format
At the end of the batch, produce a final report:
```markdown
## Batch Execution Report

### Summary
- Total agents spawned: N
- Successful: N
- Failed: N
- Total files modified: N

### Agent Details
[Table of all agents with their results]

### Integration Status
- Build: pass/fail
- Tests: pass/fail/N/A
- Lint: pass/fail/N/A

### Next Steps
[If any issues remain]
```